%N A336492 Total number of nearest-neighbor contacts over all n-step self-avoiding walks on the square lattice.
%C A336492 This sequence gives the sum of the numbers of nearest-neighbor contacts over all n-step self-avoiding walks on the square lattice. A nearest-neighbor contact is a pair of nonconsecutive vertices of a walk that are adjacent in the lattice. [Clarified by _Carlo Corti_, Aug 14 2026]
%C A336492 From _Carlo Corti_, Aug 14 2026: (Start)
%C A336492 Previous name was: Total number of neighbor contacts for n-step self-avoiding walks on a 2D square lattice.
%C A336492 The ancillary file sq_isaw_counts.ser accompanying Beaton, Guttmann, and Jensen (2020) tabulates the nonzero values c_{n,m}, the number of n-step self-avoiding walks on the square lattice with m nearest-neighbor contacts, through n = 59. (End)
%H A336492 Carlo Corti, <a href="/A336492/b336492.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..59</a>
%H A336492 N. R. Beaton, A. J. Guttmann, and I. Jensen, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.05852">Two-dimensional interacting self-avoiding walks: new estimates for critical temperatures and exponents</a>, arXiv:1911.05852 [cond-mat.stat-mech], 2020.
%H A336492 D. Bennett-Wood, I. G. Enting, D. S. Gaunt, A. J. Guttmann, J. L. Leask, A. L. Owczarek, and S. G. Whittington, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1088/0305-4470/31/20/010">Exact enumeration study of free energies of interacting polygons and walks in two dimensions</a>, J. Phys. A: Math. Gen. 31 (1998), 4725-4741.
%H A336492 M. E. Fisher and B. J. Hiley, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1731729">Configuration and free energy of a polymer molecule with solvent interaction</a>, J. Chem. Phys., 34 (1961), 1253-1267.
%H A336492 A. M. Nemirovsky, K. F. Freed, T. Ishinabe, and J. F. Douglas, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01049010">Marriage of exact enumeration and 1/d expansion methods: lattice model of dilute polymers</a>, J. Statist. Phys., 67 (1992), 1083-1108.
%H A336492 A. L. Owczarek, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1088/1742-6596/42/1/019">A note on the corrections-to-scaling for the number of nearest neighbour contacts in self-avoiding walks</a>, J. Phys.: Conf. Ser. 42 (2006), 221-224.
%F A336492 From _Carlo Corti_, Aug 14 2026: (Start)
%F A336492 Let c_{n,m} be the number of n-step self-avoiding walks on the square lattice with m nearest-neighbor contacts, and let c_n(u) = Sum_{m >= 0} c_{n,m}*u^m.
%F A336492 Then a(n) = c_n'(1) = Sum_{m >= 0} m*c_{n,m}.
%F A336492 In the notation of Owczarek (2006), m_n = a(n)/A001411(n) is the mean number of nearest-neighbor contacts among n-step self-avoiding walks. (End)
%e A336492 a(1) = a(2) = 0 as a 1 and 2 step walk cannot approach a previous step.
%e A336492 a(3) = 8. The single walk where one interaction occurs, which can be taken in eight ways on a 2D square lattice, is:
%e A336492 .
%e A336492    +---+
%e A336492        |
%e A336492    X---+
%e A336492 .
%e A336492 Therefore, the total number of interactions is 1*1*8 = 8.
%e A336492 a(4) = 32. The four walks where one interaction occurs, each of which can be taken in eight ways on a 2D square lattice, are:
%e A336492 .
%e A336492   +---+---+   +           +---+       +---+
%e A336492           |   |               |       |   |
%e A336492       X---+   +---+   X---+---+   X---+   +
%e A336492                   |
%e A336492               X---+
%e A336492 .
%e A336492 Therefore, the total number of interactions is 4*1*8 = 32.
%e A336492 a(5) = 152. Considering only walks which start with one or more steps to the right followed by an upward step there are thirty-five different walks. Eleven of these have one neighbor contact (hence A033155(5) = 11*8 = 88) while four have two contacts. These are:
%e A336492 .
%e A336492   +---+---+   +---+---+   +---+   +---+
%e A336492   |       |           |   |       |   |
%e A336492   +   X---+   X---+---+   +---+   +   +
%e A336492                               |       |
%e A336492                           X---+   X---+
%e A336492 .
%e A336492 Therefore, the total number of contacts is (11*1 + 4*2)*8 = 152.
%Y A336492 Cf. A001411 (all n-step square-lattice self-avoiding walks), A173380 (c_{n,0}), A033155 (c_{n,1}), A033323 (c_{n,2}), A038746 (corresponding equivalence classes for m=0), A038747 (corresponding equivalence classes for m=1), A047057 (simple-cubic walks with exactly one contact), A174319 (simple-cubic walks with no contacts).
